#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Wall and peak memory of jk, Gradle and Maven on one pinned public Spring Boot project.

    bench/wall/measure                         # every tool, every scenario, five timed runs each
    bench/wall/measure --tool jk --scenario edit --runs 3
    bench/wall/measure --out DIR               # rows.jsonl and the logs into DIR

The scoreboard's `wall` dimension (bench/scoreboard --tier night) runs this harness behind its quiet
gate and banks the rows in scoreboard.toml; `bench/scoreboard --render` writes the docs tables.

The project is spring-projects/spring-petclinic at the commit in PROJECT: one module, a pom.xml
and a build.gradle from the same authors, green under all three tools. Every tool gets its own
working copy under $WALL_HOME (default $JK_BENCH_HOME/wall; $JK_BENCH_HOME defaults to
${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/jk-bench), built from the clone once and then
reused, so a rerun measures and never re-provisions.

Each tool does the same work: compile the main sources, copy resources, write the plain jar. The
repository's own Gradle and Maven builds also run checkstyle, Spring Javaformat, an SBOM, Boot AOT
and Boot repackaging; those are switched off by task selection (Gradle) and skip properties (Maven)
so the row measures the build tool, not the plugins jk has no counterpart for. README.md spells
out every command.

Scenarios (tests are skipped in every wall row; `test` is its own row):
  clean    outputs gone and the tool's output cache empty for this project; the engine or daemon
           and the dependency caches stay warm — the cost of a first build on a warm machine
  rebuild  outputs gone, the tool's cache allowed: jk restores from its action cache, Gradle from
           its build cache, Maven recompiles because it has no cache
  noop     nothing changed
  edit     one leaf source file really edited (a unique comment line; jk keys on content, so a
           touch would measure a no-op), restored after each run
  test     the whole suite, forced to run: `jk test -r`, `gradle test --rerun`, `mvn test`
  idle     memory between builds: one untimed test run, IDLE_SETTLE_S with no command running, then
           the tool's residents sampled once a second — jk's engine and its warm workers, Gradle's
           daemon and its workers, nothing for Maven, which keeps no process between builds

Every timed run samples /proc every 200 ms and sums the RSS of the tool's process tree: the
command's descendants plus, for jk, the resident engine and its workers, and for Gradle the daemon
and its workers. Peak of that sum is the memory column. RSS double-counts pages shared between
JVMs, so the number is an upper bound and the same upper bound for every tool.
"""

